## 1. The Emperor, and his question

*In ancient times there was the Yellow Emperor. Born with a spirit-like
intelligence, able to speak while still an infant, quick and penetrating as a
boy, sincere and diligent as a young man, he ascended to the throne when he came
of age.*

He asked his Heavenly Master:

> 余聞上古之人，春秋皆度百歲，而動作不衰；
> 今時之人，年半百而動作皆衰者，時世異耶？人將失之耶？

*"**I have heard** that the people of high antiquity all passed a hundred springs
and autumns, and their movements did not weaken. People of the present age are
worn out in their movements at half a hundred years. Is it that the times are
different — or is it that people have lost something?"*

**Note.** The chapter opens with 余聞, *"I have heard."* The claim that the
ancients reached a hundred years is marked as report, not observation, by the
speaker making it — and the question that follows is a genuine question, not a
rhetorical one.

---

## 2. The physician's answer: those who knew the Way

Qi Bo replied:

> 上古之人，其知道者，法於陰陽，和於術數，
> 食飲有節，起居有常，不妄作勞，
> 故能形與神俱，而盡終其天年，度百歲乃去。

*"Among the people of high antiquity, **those who knew the Way** patterned
themselves on yin and yang, kept in accord with the arts of number, ate and drank
with measure, rose and rested at regular hours, and did not labour recklessly.
So form and spirit were whole together, and they lived out the years heaven
allotted them, departing only after a hundred."*

**Note.** 其知道者 — *those among them who knew the Way* — is a restrictive
clause. The claim is about a subset, not about everyone in antiquity.

> 今時之人不然也，以酒為漿，以妄為常，醉以入房，
> 以欲竭其精，以耗散其真，不知持滿，不時御神，
> 務快其心，逆於生樂，起居無節，故半百而衰也。

*"People of the present age are not like this. They take wine for water and take
the reckless for the ordinary; they go to the bedchamber intoxicated, and through
appetite exhaust their essence and scatter what is true in them. They do not know
how to hold fullness; they do not govern the spirit in season. They hurry after
what pleases the heart, turning against the real joy of living, and keep no
regularity in rising and resting. And so they are spent at half a hundred."*

---

## 3. What the sages taught

> 夫上古聖人之教下也，皆謂之虛邪賊風，避之有時，
> 恬惔虛無，真氣從之，精神內守，病安從來。

*"When the sages of high antiquity taught those below them, they all spoke of
avoiding, in their seasons, the depleting influences and the injurious winds —
and of remaining calm and empty of craving, so that the true qi follows, essence
and spirit are kept within, and sickness has nowhere to come from."*

**Note.** *"Sickness has nowhere to come from"* (病安從來) is phrased as a
rhetorical question in the Chinese. It is the preventive claim of the chapter, and
the remaining eighty chapters of the Su Wen proceed to answer it literally.

> 是以志閑而少欲，心安而不懼，形勞而不倦，氣從以順，
> 各從其欲，皆得所願。

*"Their aims were unhurried and their wants were few; their hearts were at ease
and unafraid; their bodies worked without exhaustion. Qi moved with them
compliantly, each followed what he wished, and all obtained what they hoped for."*

> 故美其食，任其服，樂其俗，高下不相慕，其民故曰樸。

*"So they found their food good, were content with their clothes, took pleasure
in their own customs, and high and low did not envy one another. Such people were
therefore called **simple** — unworked, like uncarved wood."*

> 是以嗜欲不能勞其目，淫邪不能惑其心，
> 愚智賢不肖，不懼於物，故合於道。

*"Craving could not tire their eyes, nor excess confuse their hearts. Foolish or
wise, worthy or unworthy, they did not fear circumstance — and so they accorded
with the Way."*

> 所以能年皆度百歲而動作不衰者，以其德全不危也。

*"The reason they could all pass a hundred years without their movements
weakening is that their inner power was whole and undamaged."*

